## Frame ownership and dispatch

`bench-frame-view` is mostly an assertion made visible: that same steady-state delivery
costs exactly one allocation, the owned-frame copy, gated at `== 1`. It also exercises the
owned-`Frame` to borrowed-`FrameView` projection a million times as a correctness check, and
reports the protocol model's struct sizes, the inputs to the footprint math above. It does
not time the projection. Measure your own: `zig build bench-frame-view -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.

```=html
<!-- bench:frame-view GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<table>
<tr><th>metric</th><th>value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Allocations per delivered turn</td><td>1 (gated <code>== 1</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frame-view projections exercised</td><td>1,000,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Argument / Message struct bytes</td><td>88 / 208</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reference / Resolution struct bytes</td><td>80 / 96</td></tr>
</table>
```

## Encrypted transport round-trip

`bench-iroh` times one full OCapN call — a `greet` out, the resolve back — over a
live socket, on the same machine, over the encrypted
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) transport's keyed QUIC and over the cleartext
`tcp` transport as a baseline.

The first two charts are the **serial** case: one connection, one call in flight,
no pipelining, so each figure is the wire's round-trip latency and the throughput is
its reciprocal. This is the worst case for the iroh path. The sidecar
runs QUIC on its own tokio runtime, and the synchronous driver blocks the calling
thread on each read, so every serial round-trip pays a cross-thread wake with
nothing to amortize it. Most of the gap to TCP's in-thread blocking socket is that
scheduling hop, not the AEAD encryption, which is sub-microsecond for a frame this
small. Read
the serial latency as latency, not as the transport's throughput ceiling.

The last chart shows why that ceiling is illusory. On one readiness loop
(ADR-0057, the `iroh-loop` path) the per-connection round-trips overlap instead
of each blocking the next, so one thread sustains several times the single-stream
throughput.

Read none of this as field latency: both transports run over loopback, which removes
the propagation delay and packet loss a relayed or hole-punched path on the open
internet would add. Measure your own: `zig build bench-iroh` needs `cargo` for
the iroh sidecar.

```=html
<!-- bench:iroh GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<div class="bench-legend"><span><i style="background:#257f88"></i>TCP (cleartext baseline)</span><span><i style="background:#c76f18"></i>iroh (encrypted QUIC)</span></div><div class="bench-charts">
