## The same app on pancake and Goblins

pancake's Racket binding and [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) both
speak OCapN, so one Racket application runs unchanged on either and can be timed head to
head. `zig build bench-goblins` does exactly that: one application source, four workloads
(an echo, a capability registry, a mint and purse, and a task board). Each workload is driven
identically, with synchronous round-trips and no promise pipelining on either side, over
loopback TCP with the same default socket options. Each run warms the path, forces a collection, and samples the
live set; the charts report p50 (the median round-trip) and throughput in requests per
second. The
[suite README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bench/goblins/README.md)
writes the fairness model down in full. Measure your own: `zig build bench-goblins`
needs `racket` and the `goblins` collection on `PATH`; append `-- --smoke` for a fast pass.

Read the pancake bars carefully: they are not pancake's Zig actor runtime. The
application's actors run inside the Racket binding's own small vat; what pancake supplies
underneath is the wire, the OCapN session and codec, compiled to a native library and
driven over the C ABI. So this pits pancake's protocol core against Goblins', under one
Racket application and actor style. It does not measure native Zig actors, the production
network layer, or many connections at once; those live in other benchmarks. Within those
bounds, pancake's wire core is faster than Goblins' on all four workloads.

The third series, **pancake + time-travel**, is that same binding with the snapshot feature
turned on for every actor, so the vat keeps a revisable history of all of them. It answers a
fair question: if you pay for time-travel everywhere, what does it cost? On the light
workloads it lands within noise of plain pancake; on the heavier, more stateful ones it
costs a real slice of throughput, plus the memory the vat spends holding that history. The
section below measures the snapshot operation itself, away from the wire.

```=html
