## Time-travel: snapshot and restore

pancake can take a snapshot of a vat's local actors and rewind to it later. It is the same
local time-travel Spritely Goblins offers, with one difference: in pancake the feature is off
by default, turned on per actor, so an actor you never snapshot pays nothing for it.

Taking a snapshot is cheap because it shares each actor's state instead of copying it. The cost
grows with the number of actors you have turned the feature on for, not with how much each one
holds: snapshotting a thousand actors costs about the same whether each holds 64 bytes or 16 KB.
The charts below put pancake's snapshot and restore beside Goblins' across a range of actor
counts. Both share state the same way, so the bars show that shared shape, not a winner.
pancake runs in Zig and Goblins in Racket. pancake's native code shows lower numbers at most
points, but the two land close on restore at the largest counts, where the ordering can even
flip.

Measure your own with `zig build bench-snapshot` and `racket bench/goblins/snapshot.rkt`. Those
benchmarks also answer a deeper question: what it costs to change a snapshotted actor as the
data it holds grows larger.

